Validated 1 entities; 0 errors, 0 gaps
Body: 0 words
Lens single: One statement
  Does the body state exactly one thing? An "and", a bulleted list of behaviours, or
  two sentences each with their own verb mean this is two requirements. Split it —
  two records each with their own `realized_in` are worth more than one that cannot
  be satisfied or violated as a unit.
Lens testable: Observable from outside
  Could someone who did not write this tell whether the system satisfies it, without
  reading the implementation? If the only way to check is to open the code, this is
  describing a design, not stating a requirement.
Lens measurable: A number and its measurement
  Look for the value, the unit, where it is measured, over what window, and what
  enforces it. Flag "gracefully", "robust", "scalable", "efficient", "fast",
  "user-friendly" — an adjective is not a bound. "p99 under 200 ms at the API edge,
  measured over a rolling hour, alerted on in the latency dashboard" is.
Lens realized: Where it is satisfied
  `realized_in` is the only edge tying a rule to the code that satisfies it, and a
  requirement without one is unfinished by design. If no component satisfies it yet,
  that is the honest answer and the gap is the point — but check that it is not
  simply unlinked. If a use case depends on this rule, name it in `use_cases`: a
  rule no flow needs is either global or unowned, and the graph should say which.
